# Part 4: Revisions

## 8. Revision kya hota hai?

Replica aur Revision ko confuse nahi karna.

Maan lo humne OrderService deploy kiya hai.

Initially humare paas version 1 hai:

```text
order-service:v1
```

Ab humne application mein kuch changes kiye:

- New API endpoint add kiya.
- Business logic update ki.
- Bug fix kiya.

Ab humne updated Docker Image build ki:

```text
order-service:v2
```

Jab hum updated version deploy karte hain, Azure Container Apps ek new Revision create kar sakta hai.

**Revision ka matlab hai Container App ka ek specific deployed version aur configuration.**

Example:

```text
             OrderService Container App
                         |
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              |                     |
              v                     v
         Revision 1             Revision 2
         (Old Version)          (New Version)
              |                     |
        order-service:v1       order-service:v2
              |                     |
         ┌────┴────┐           ┌────┴────┐
         |         |           |         |
         v         v           v         v
      Replica 1 Replica 2   Replica 1 Replica 2
```

Dono revisions ko simultaneously run karna possible hai.

## 9. Traffic Splitting kya hota hai?

Azure Container Apps mein revisions ke beech traffic distribute kar sakte hain.

Example:

Maan lo humne OrderService ka new version deploy kiya hai.

Lekin hum immediately 100% traffic new version ko nahi dena chahte.

Hum traffic splitting configure kar sakte hain:

```text
Revision 1 (Old Version) = 10% Traffic

Revision 2 (New Version) = 90% Traffic
```

Iska benefit:

- New version ko gradually test kar sakte hain.
- Old version ko available rakh sakte hain.
- Problems hone par traffic old version par shift kar sakte hain.

**Remember:**

| Concept | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Container App | Deployed application |
| Replica | Same version ka running instance |
| Revision | Application ka specific deployed version/configuration |

