# Part 7: Real E-Commerce Project Example

Ab apne E-Commerce Microservices Project ke basis par samajhte hain.

Humare paas:

```text
E-Commerce Project
│
├── ProductService
│   └── .NET Web API + MySQL
│
├── OrderService
│   └── .NET Web API + MongoDB
│
├── UserService
│   └── .NET Web API + PostgreSQL
│
├── Ocelot API Gateway
├── Redis
└── RabbitMQ
```

## 14. Agar Azure App Service use karein

Hum ek App Service Plan create karenge.

Uske andar separate Web Apps create karenge:

```text
App Service Plan
│
├── ProductService-WebApp
├── OrderService-WebApp
├── UserService-WebApp
└── Gateway-WebApp
```

Har Web App mein separately application deploy karenge.

Hum published .NET files ya custom Docker Images use kar sakte hain.

## 15. Agar Azure Container Apps use karein

Hum ek Container Apps Environment create karenge.

Uske andar separate Container Apps create karenge:

```text
ECommerce-Environment
│
├── ProductService-ContainerApp
│   └── ProductService Docker Image
│
├── OrderService-ContainerApp
│   └── OrderService Docker Image
│
├── UserService-ContainerApp
│   └── UserService Docker Image
│
└── Gateway-ContainerApp
    └── Ocelot Docker Image
```

Har Container App apne according replicas increase ya decrease kar sakta hai.

Example:

```text
ProductService = 1 Replica

OrderService = 5 Replicas

UserService = 1 Replica

Gateway = 2 Replicas
```

Agar OrderService par traffic increase hota hai, toh hum uske replicas increase kar sakte hain bina ProductService aur UserService ke replica count ko change kiye.

**Note:** Ye example application replicas ka hai. Redis, RabbitMQ aur databases ko bhi separately host karna padega, jaise managed Azure services ya suitable container hosting. Unhe automatically Container Apps Environment create karne se deploy nahi kiya jata.

